{"edits":[{"content":"The proposed approach identifies the accepted set as the largest sub-relation conforming to the school's stance within an operator lattice. Yet, locating such a maximal sub-relation that satisfies a monotonicity or stance property in abstract argumentation is equivalent to locating a maximal admissible set, a task that is NP-complete. This directly contradicts the requirement that the algorithm preserve polynomial-time cost, as the lattice traversal cannot be guaranteed to complete in polynomial time in the worst case.\n\nThe critic's objection does not hold against the proposal as stated. The critic unjustifiably equates 'finding a maximal sub-relation satisfying a monotonicity or stance property' with 'finding a maximal admissible set' and then infers NP-completeness, but this equivalence is neither proven nor implied by the target. The target does not demand the computation of an arbitrary maximal admissible set in an argumentation framework. Rather, it specifies a finite family of monotone fixed-point operators and a single monotone pass over the operator lattice to select the operator whose accepted set is the maximal sub-relation meeting the school's stance. A monotone fixed-point operator on a finite lattice reaches its least fixed point via polynomially many ascending steps in the lattice height; selecting among a finite set of such operators, given a committed school input, is a bounded choice, not a combinatorial search over all admissible sets. The NP-completeness charge would apply to computing maximal admissible sets under admissibility semantics for an arbitrary framework, but the target replaces that search with an explicit, committed selector over monotone operators. The critic does not show that the operator lattice has exponential height or that the selector must enumerate an exponential candidate space; absent that, no worst-case exponential lower bound follows. The critic's second point, that 'maximal sub-relation satisfying a stance' lacks a canonical ordering over relations, also fails to demonstrate a flaw: maximality in a finite lattice is a lattice-theoretic notion independent of any external ordering; a greatest or maximal element satisfying a property is defined by the lattice's partial order, not by a total order. Determinism is preserved because the school is a first-class input and the selector is evaluated per committed school over the finite monotone operator family. The critic's concern that hardness might be displaced rather than eliminated is merely abstract; displacement would require a reduction showing the selector or the operator-lattice pass hides an NP-hard subproblem, which the critic does not provide. Nothing in the target's specification entails that the operator-lattice pass or the finite selection is worst-case non-polynomial, and the critic has not established otherwise. Hence, the polynomial cost guarantee is not shown to be violated."},{"content":"The proposal's method for deriving the accepted set is to take the maximal sub-relation that respects the school's stance, operating over a lattice of operators. The issue is that determining such a maximal sub-relation satisfying a monotonicity or stance condition in abstract argumentation is tantamount to determining a maximal admissible set, which is NP-complete. This runs afoul of the explicit demand that the cost remain polynomial, because the search over the operator lattice cannot be performed in polynomial time in the worst case.\n\nThe critic's case does not succeed against the target as specified. The critic conflates 'finding a maximal sub-relation satisfying a monotonicity or stance property' with 'finding a maximal admissible set' and declares NP-completeness, but this conflation is not substantiated and is not required by the target. The target does not require computing an arbitrary maximal admissible set of an argumentation framework; it defines a finite collection of monotone fixed-point operators and a single monotone sweep over the operator lattice to choose the operator whose accepted set is the maximal sub-relation satisfying the school's stance. A monotone fixed-point operator on a finite lattice converges to its least fixed point after a polynomial number of ascending iterations based on lattice height; the selection among a finite family of such operators, under a committed school input, is a finite bounded choice rather than a combinatorial search over all admissible sets. The critic's NP-completeness claim would apply to maximal admissible sets of an arbitrary framework under admissibility semantics, but the target substitutes that search with an explicit, committed selector over monotone operators. The critic does not demonstrate that the operator lattice has exponential height or that the selector enumerates an exponential candidate space; without such evidence, no exponential lower bound is warranted. The critic's additional assertion that 'maximal sub-relation satisfying a stance' is ill-defined without a canonical ordering over relations also does not establish a defect: maximality in a finite lattice is a lattice-theoretic concept independent of any external ordering; a greatest or maximal element satisfying a property is determined by the partial order of the lattice, not by a canonical total order. The target's determinism is preserved because the school is committed as first-class input, and the selector is evaluated per committed school over the finite monotone operator family. The critic at most raises an abstract worry that hardness could be displaced rather than removed, but displacement would require showing that the selector or the operator-lattice pass hides an equivalent NP-hard subproblem; the critic provides no such reduction. Nothing in the target's specification entails that the operator-lattice pass or the finite selection is worst-case non-polynomial, and the critic has not established otherwise. Therefore, the polynomial cost guarantee is not shown to be violated."}]}